            <ArticleTitle>Psoriasis and hypertension: a case-control study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>785-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1468-3083.2011.04108.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">  Several studies stated that patient with psoriasis carried an increased risk of psoriasis but some studies did not demonstrate this association.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">  The aim of this study was to evaluate the prevalence of hypertension in psoriasis based on a sample of Spanish population.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">  This was a hospital-based case-control study involving 661 psoriatic patients (cases) and 661 control matched by gender and age. Meta-analysis of the previous studies was made.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">  The prevalence of hypertension was significantly higher in psoriasis patients than controls (30.3%, 21.3%, respectively, P &lt; 0.001). In a multivariate analysis, hypertension was associated with psoriasis after controlling for age, gender, diabetes, obesity and smoking (OR = 1.44, 95% confidence interval: 1.07-1.94).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">  The results of this study support the association between psoriasis and hypertension.</AbstractText>
